  "caveats": [
    {
      "id": "loadgen",
      "text": "The load generator was a 4 vCPU droplet, same size as the app. At crocket's max it was only around half busy (JSON about 50%, plaintext about 55%), so I wouldn't call those numbers client-pegged, though a larger generator could still move them"
    },
    {
      "id": "drogon-db",
      "text": "Drogon does beat crocket on the database test, 49,232 req/s against 45,453, and that's real. It also used about 201 MB of RSS under that load, against 32 MB for crocket"
    },
    {
      "id": "fastapi-rate",
      "text": "FastAPI couldn't hold the shared rate on any of the three tests, so a p99 at that rate would just be queueing. The table keeps that flag, and shows latency at a rate it can hold instead, about 60% of its max. It ran four uvicorn workers, one per core"
    },
    {
      "id": "fastify-single-process",
      "text": "Fastify runs as a single Node process, by design, so it tops out around one core. CPU per request is the fairer comparison there"
    },
    {
      "id": "spring-histogram",
      "text": "Spring Boot needs a one-line property to expose histogram buckets at /metrics. That setting is on for these numbers"
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "fastapiHeld": "FastAPI tops out around 6,000 req/s on these 4 vCPUs, so at the shared 10,000 (or 4,000 for the query test) requests just queue up. Those wouldn't be fair p50 or p99 figures, which is why the table flags them. The numbers you see there are from a rate it can hold, about 60% of its max: 3,500 req/s for JSON, 3,600 for plaintext, and 2,400 for the single query",
    "coldStart": "Cold start is container start to the first 200 on /db. That's the number you feel when a new pod comes up during autoscaling or a deploy. Spring Boot takes about 4\u20135 seconds here; crocket is around 330 milliseconds"
  },
